And we are going on. In this lesson

6
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:17,850
we are talking about modals, modal verbs.

7
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:25,680
You know, modal verbs are verbs like should, could, can must, may and so on.

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:33,750
However, in this lesson we are only talking about modals of probability, so how to talk about probability

9
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:35,070
or possibility.

10
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:45,570
And we are going to look on the usage of those verbs, modal verbs in present and in the past.

11
00:00:45,570 --> 00:00:52,230
So the first section of the lesson is going to be about the present and the second section about the

12
00:00:52,230 --> 00:00:52,740
past.

13
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:54,900
So let's look at it.

14
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:04,290
Which modal verbs do we do we use when we need to express how certain we are about something?

15
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:12,120
Here are those words must, might, could, may, can't plus infinitive.

16
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:16,080
So these modal verbs plus infinitive.

17
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:21,360
You remember that you don't use anything between the modal verb and the infinitive.

18
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:21,630
No to.

19
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:32,460
Nothing. For example, could go or can't play or must learn something like that.

20
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:32,660
Yeah.

21
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:36,630
So the modal verb and the infinitive.

22
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:44,130
And here on the screen you can see the pretty little table.

23
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:51,690
In the first column of this table, there is a modal verb, and in the second column there is the meaning

24
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:57,780
or which probability does this modal verb express.

25
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:00,150
So let's look at them.

26
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:09,000
Might, may in could these modal verbs, when you use them in the sentence, express that you think that

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,160
something is possible?

28
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:13,260
We say it's possible.

29
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:14,670
I am not sure.

30
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:18,450
It's not certain, but it's possible.

31
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:24,060
And here in brackets, let's say it's 50% certainty.

32
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,910
Maybe yes, maybe no, maybe it is like that.

33
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:27,750
Maybe not.

34
00:02:28,890 --> 00:02:38,000
The next one is must, must is a strong verb, and in probability must means it's probable.

35
00:02:38,010 --> 00:02:41,250
Or we can even say it's very, very probable.

36
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,010
It means, I am sure about it.

37
00:02:44,010 --> 00:02:47,430
I am, let's say, 99% sure.

38
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:57,570
I'm very certain that something is true. And can't is also a very strong moral word.

39
00:02:57,660 --> 00:02:59,640
But it means the opposite.

40
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:01,830
It means it's impossible.

41
00:03:01,830 --> 00:03:06,570
So again, 99% certainty, but the opposite.

42
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:07,560
It's impossible.

43
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:08,760
I don't believe it.

44
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:09,390
No way 

45
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:10,350
it's like that.

46
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,710
So we have might, may, could -

47
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:14,730
it's possible.

48
00:03:14,730 --> 00:03:15,450
Must -

49
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:18,210
it's very possible and can't -

50
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,140
no way,

51
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:20,310
it's not possible.

52
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:21,970
All right.

53
00:03:21,970 --> 00:03:26,050
So let's look at some examples to see it more clearly.

54
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,010
So Tony isn't answering my calls.

55
00:03:31,030 --> 00:03:33,150
He might be in a meeting now.

56
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:36,690
Remember, we are speaking about the present. 

57
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:37,150
Yes.

58
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:42,000
Now, right now, I think he might be in the meeting.

59
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:43,000
I'm not sure.

60
00:03:43,510 --> 00:03:44,470
It's 50%.

61
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:44,830
Maybe 

62
00:03:44,830 --> 00:03:45,880
yes, maybe no.

63
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:47,530
Maybe he is in the meeting now.

64
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:48,520
It's possible.

65
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:53,780
Veronica is our new back-end developer.

66
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:57,500
She might not be familiar with all of the tools we use.

67
00:03:58,070 --> 00:03:59,870
Again, it means

68
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:01,380
maybe

69
00:04:01,380 --> 00:04:02,760
yes, maybe no.

70
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,080
I'm not certain.

71
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:07,410
So maybe she's not familiar with those tools.

72
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:08,820
It's possible.

73
00:04:10,470 --> 00:04:12,810
So let's look at may as well.

74
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,820
Ask Chao, he's a fullstack developer.

75
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,899
He may quickly find the solution to your problem.

76
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:22,180
So the meaning is the same as

77
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:23,680
as with might.

78
00:04:23,740 --> 00:04:26,080
Maybe you will find the solution quickly.

79
00:04:26,080 --> 00:04:26,980
It's possible.

80
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:30,790
I'm surprised that Lucas hasn't joined.

81
00:04:30,820 --> 00:04:31,900
He may not know

82
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:35,650
the meeting is today. Okay, speaking about the present.

83
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:38,800
Maybe he doesn't know that the meeting is today.

84
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:40,090
So might,

85
00:04:40,090 --> 00:04:43,960
may are the same and could as well.

86
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:45,790
But with could, we use

87
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:51,370
could only in positive sentences instead of might or instead of may.

88
00:04:52,780 --> 00:04:54,280
So let's compare.

89
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,550
Tony isn't answering my calls.

90
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,450
He might be in the meeting now.

91
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:01,270
Exactly the same meaning would be:

92
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:03,570
Toni isn't answering my calls.

93
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:05,420
He could be in the meeting now.

94
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:07,960
So this could.

95
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:09,140
Yes, could

96
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:11,380
is the past tense of

97
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:13,150
can, you know that,

98
00:05:13,910 --> 00:05:21,800
but in this meaning for probability, to express probability or possibility we use could in the present

99
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,630
and it means in the present.

100
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:26,270
He could be in the meeting right now.

101
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:28,810
Good.

102
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:30,470
Let's move on to

103
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:33,200
to must.

104
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:35,530
My neighbors must be out of town.

105
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:40,340
I haven't seen them for a week. Because I haven't seen them for a week

106
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:50,840
I am pretty sure that they are out of town, because otherwise I would see them every day or at least

107
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:52,460
once in a week.

108
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:59,000
So I'm pretty sure, as we said, 99% or 90% sure that they are out of town.

109
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,650
That's why I use this strong modal verb must.

110
00:06:03,950 --> 00:06:08,480
And it would sound something like, I'm sure they're out of town.

111
00:06:10,090 --> 00:06:11,440
Another example.

112
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:13,630
Andrii must earn a lot.

113
00:06:13,660 --> 00:06:16,000
He's a senior developer in our company.

114
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:18,340
Again, I'm pretty sure about it.

115
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:19,990
I'm pretty certain.

116
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,000
I'm sure he earns a lot.

117
00:06:22,030 --> 00:06:25,120
That's why I use the word must. Remember

118
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:30,670
it's a strong one. And the opposite of most is can't.

119
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:33,190
Can't means "no way",

120
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:35,050
I don't believe it.

121
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,260
That man can't be Daniela's husband.

122
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:39,760
Her husband is older.

123
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:41,290
Okay. 

124
00:06:41,430 --> 00:06:44,300
I mean, it's impossible that he's her husband.

125
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:45,520
I don't believe it.

126
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:46,170
No way

127
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:46,980
he's her husband.

128
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:50,860
This can't be a local issue.

129
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:56,680
The program fails on every device. Because the program fails on every device

130
00:06:57,070 --> 00:07:01,300
it's very probable that it's not a local issue.

131
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:02,500
So it's impossible

132
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:03,820
it's a local issue.

133
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:05,140
Good.

134
00:07:05,170 --> 00:07:08,440
So these were modal verbs.

135
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:13,060
Might, may, could, must and can't in the present.

136
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:22,090
Now, let's jump to an a little bit bigger table here and let's see how we use those modals of probability

137
00:07:22,090 --> 00:07:24,940
for the past. And

138
00:07:26,010 --> 00:07:30,120
for the past, the formation is just a little bit more difficult.

139
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:31,470
So let's see.

140
00:07:31,470 --> 00:07:35,160
We take those same modals.

141
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:38,130
Must, might, could, may, can't.

142
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:40,650
And then we add have.

143
00:07:41,990 --> 00:07:44,960
And then we add past participle.

144
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:48,530
So must have and past participle.

145
00:07:48,950 --> 00:07:51,520
Past participle is the verb with -ed.

146
00:07:52,330 --> 00:08:02,800
Or it's the third column of our irregular verbs, for example, done, yes, must have done or might have

147
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:07,210
opened or could have been.

148
00:08:08,410 --> 00:08:09,880
So this is the formation.

149
00:08:11,050 --> 00:08:18,940
And to look at this table a little bit easier, we can play a game here.

150
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:29,120
For example, we are at a meeting and Sarah was also supposed to join, but she didn't join.

151
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:36,190
And now we're at the meeting and we are thinking, what could have happened that she didn't join.

152
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,510
So the question is, why isn't Sarah at the meeting?

153
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:43,600
And we look at the options.

154
00:08:44,310 --> 00:08:46,830
And we know that it's in the past.

155
00:08:47,130 --> 00:08:53,460
So all of the options that we are discussing, it's something that happened in the past.

156
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:54,690
So let's see.

157
00:08:54,690 --> 00:09:00,330
The first example is Sarah might have forgotten about the meeting.

158
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:02,220
What does it mean?

159
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:08,250
She's not here because she might have forgotten about the meeting.

160
00:09:08,370 --> 00:09:10,770
It means it's possible.

161
00:09:10,770 --> 00:09:14,160
We're not sure, but it's possible. And the formation is

162
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:18,090
might have forgotten. Another option.

163
00:09:18,480 --> 00:09:20,880
She might not have felt well.

164
00:09:21,990 --> 00:09:23,490
Okay, again

165
00:09:23,490 --> 00:09:24,570
it's possible.

166
00:09:25,490 --> 00:09:28,220
And we use not here for negative form.

167
00:09:29,160 --> 00:09:29,640
Good.

168
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:33,140
We have a star here an asterisk. 

169
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:41,580
It means that we can use this formation not only with might, but, as you know, also with may and could.

170
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:42,810
So

171
00:09:42,810 --> 00:09:45,660
could have forgotten or may have forgotten.

172
00:09:48,290 --> 00:09:49,520
The next option.

173
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:53,420
She must have had an overlapping meeting.

174
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:59,600
Now here I am pretty sure, it's very probable. Because she's so busy

175
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:02,510
she always has a lot of meetings during the day.

176
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:10,340
And I'm pretty sure that she has two meetings overlapping so at the same time or partly at the same

177
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:10,700
time.

178
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:15,740
So probably she's at another meeting, so she couldn't join this one.

179
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:18,170
I'm sure about it.

180
00:10:18,190 --> 00:10:20,560
That's why I use must have had.

181
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:30,550
And the last option would be Sarah can't have ignored the meeting. And it means I don't believe it.

182
00:10:30,790 --> 00:10:32,230
She's very responsible.

183
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:38,470
She always joins the meetings so she can have forgotten or she can have ignored the meeting.

184
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:40,450
It means it's impossible.

185
00:10:40,450 --> 00:10:41,050
No way.

186
00:10:41,050 --> 00:10:42,100
I don't believe it.

187
00:10:44,210 --> 00:10:44,470
Okay, good.

188
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,740
So this is the

189
00:10:47,740 --> 00:10:50,320
the whole theory for today, for this lesson.

190
00:10:50,530 --> 00:10:51,790
Nice and easy.

